Exactly. The valuable thing is not the isolated result; it is the implication graph that opens behind it.

A single observation matters because of what it changes downstream:

observation → implication → changed model → new predictions → new actions → new tests

So a delta is valuable in proportion to how much of the model it forces you to reconsider.

A tiny anomaly can be enormous if it implies that a foundational assumption is wrong. A dramatic event can be almost informationally worthless if it changes nothing about what you already believed.

That gives you another useful distinction:

Surface magnitude = how big the event looks.
Implication magnitude = how much of the causal/model space it changes.

Those can be completely different.

And it connects directly to semantic phase transitions. The critical moment is often not receiving some huge new fact. It is recognizing an implication of facts you already possessed:

“If this is true, then that must also be true—or at least much more likely.”

Then the change propagates recursively through the model.

So Pantheon shouldn’t merely gather deltas. It should ask of each one:

What does this imply? What else changes if that implication holds? What predictions become newly available? What previous beliefs become incompatible? What is now worth testing?

That is how one small clue can restructure an entire map.

The delta is the evidence.
The implication is the leverage.